<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorrin A. Thurston</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1858–1931</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorrin A. Thurston was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> lawyer and publisher, grandson of American missionaries, who led the Committee of Safety that overthrew Queen Liliʻuokalani in January 1893. Earlier he had helped force the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a> on King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> in 1887. He lobbied in Washington for annexation, achieved in 1898, and later owned the Honolulu newspaper the Pacific Commercial Advertiser.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston.jpg" alt="Lorrin A. Thurston" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorrin_A._Thurston,_1892.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
